{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"Atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia is a disease in which too many granulocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia is a disease in which too many granulocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"In atypical CML, the body tells too many blood stem cells to become granulocytes; some never mature and are called blasts.","source_span":"the body tells too many blood stem cells to become a type of white blood cell called granulocytes. Some of these blood stem cells never become mature white blood cells. These immature white blood cells are called blasts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"Over time, granulocytes and blasts crowd out red blood cells and platelets in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Over time, the granulocytes and blasts crowd out the red blood cells and platelets in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"Leukemia cells in atypical CML look like those in CML under a microscope, but atypical CML lacks the Philadelphia chromosome change.","source_span":"in atypical CML a certain chromosome change, called the \"Philadelphia chromosome\" is not there."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"Signs and symptoms of atypical CML include easy bruising or bleeding and feeling tired and weak.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia include easy bruising or bleeding and feeling tired and weak."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"Symptoms to check with a doctor about include shortness of breath, pale skin, petechiae, and pain or fullness below the ribs on the left side.","source_span":"Shortness of breath. - Pale skin. - Feeling very tired and weak. - Easy bruising or bleeding. - Petechiae (flat, pinpoint spots under the skin caused by bleeding). - Pain or a feeling of fullness below the ribs on the left side."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"The prognosis for atypical CML depends on the number of red blood cells and platelets in the blood.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) for atypical CML depends on the number of red blood cells and platelets in the blood."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","statement":"Treatment of atypical CML may include chemotherapy.","source_span":"Treatment of atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) may include chemotherapy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia?","answer":"It is a disease in which too many granulocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How does atypical CML differ genetically from typical CML even though the cells look alike under a microscope?","answer":"In atypical CML, the leukemia cells look the same as in CML under a microscope, but atypical CML does not have the Philadelphia chromosome change that CML has."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What happens to red blood cells and platelets over time in atypical CML?","answer":"Over time, the excess granulocytes and blasts crowd out the red blood cells and platelets in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What symptoms should prompt someone to check with a doctor about possible atypical CML?","answer":"Shortness of breath, pale skin, feeling very tired and weak, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, and pain or fullness below the ribs on the left side."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What factor affects the prognosis (chance of recovery) for atypical CML?","answer":"The prognosis depends on the number of red blood cells and platelets in the blood."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What treatment may be used for atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia?","answer":"Treatment of atypical CML may include chemotherapy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","statement":"MDS/MPN-UC has features of both myelodysplastic and myeloproliferative diseases but is not chronic myelomonocytic leukemia, juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia, or atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia.","source_span":"is a disease that has features of both myelodysplastic and myeloproliferative diseases but is not chronic myelomonocytic leukemia, juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia, or atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","statement":"Signs and symptoms of MDS/MPN-UC include fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm, unclassifiable, include fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","statement":"In MDS/MPN-UC, blood stem cells are told to become red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets, and some never mature, becoming blasts that crowd out healthy blood cells.","source_span":"the body tells too many blood stem cells to become red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets. Some of these blood stem cells never become mature blood cells. These immature blood cells are called blasts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","statement":"MDS/MPN-UC is a very rare disease, so the factors that affect risk and prognosis are not known.","source_span":"MDS/MPN-UC is a very rare disease. Because it is so rare, the factors that affect risk and prognosis are not known."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","statement":"Symptoms to check with a doctor about include fever or frequent infections, shortness of breath, pale skin, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, and pain or fullness below the ribs.","source_span":"Fever or frequent infections. - Shortness of breath. - Feeling very tired and weak. - Pale skin. - Easy bruising or bleeding. - Petechiae (flat, pinpoint spots under the skin caused by bleeding). - Pain or a feeling of fullness below the ribs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","statement":"Treatment of MDS/MPN-UC may include supportive care and targeted therapy with imatinib mesylate.","source_span":"Supportive care treatments to manage problems caused by the disease such as infection, bleeding, and anemia. - Targeted therapy (imatinib mesylate)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm, unclassifiable (MDS/MPN-UC)?","answer":"It is a disease with features of both myelodysplastic and myeloproliferative diseases, but it is not chronic myelomonocytic leukemia, juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia, or atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[1]}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the main signs and symptoms of MDS/MPN-UC?","answer":"Fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Why are the risk factors and prognosis factors for MDS/MPN-UC not well known?","answer":"Because MDS/MPN-UC is a very rare disease, the factors that affect its risk and prognosis are not known."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What targeted therapy drug may be used to treat MDS/MPN-UC?","answer":"Imatinib mesylate may be used as targeted therapy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Besides targeted therapy, what other kind of treatment may be given for MDS/MPN-UC?","answer":"Supportive care treatments to manage problems such as infection, bleeding, and anemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_4","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What symptom involving infections should prompt a doctor visit for possible MDS/MPN-UC?","answer":"Fever or frequent infections is one of the symptoms to check with a doctor about."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Endometrial cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the endometrium.","source_span":"Endometrial cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the endometrium."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Obesity and having metabolic syndrome may increase the risk of endometrial cancer.","source_span":"Obesity and having metabolic syndrome may increase the risk of endometrial cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Metabolic syndrome includes extra fat around the abdomen, high blood sugar, high blood pressure, high triglycerides, and low HDL levels in the blood.","source_span":"Having metabolic syndrome, a set of conditions that occur together, including extra fat around the abdomen, high blood sugar, high blood pressure, high levels of triglycerides and low levels of high-density lipoproteins in the blood."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Endometrial cancer may develop in breast cancer patients who have been treated with tamoxifen.","source_span":"Endometrial cancer may develop in breast cancer patients who have been treated with tamoxifen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Women taking estrogen alone (without progesterone) have an increased risk of endometrial cancer, but taking estrogen combined with progesterone does not increase that risk.","source_span":"Women taking estrogen (a hormone that can affect the growth of some cancers) alone also have an increased risk of endometrial cancer. Taking estrogen combined with progesterone (another hormone) does not increase a womans risk of endometrial cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Other risk factors for endometrial cancer include endometrial hyperplasia, never giving birth, early menstruation, late menopause, PCOS, family history of uterine cancer, a Lynch syndrome-linked gene change, and hyperinsulinemia.","source_span":"Having endometrial hyperplasia. - Being obese. ... Never giving birth. - Beginning menstruation at an early age. - Reaching menopause at an older age. - Having polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS). - Having a mother, sister, or daughter with uterine cancer. - Having a certain gene change that is linked to Lynch syndrome (hereditary non-polyposis colon cancer). - Having hyperinsulinemia (high levels of insulin in the blood)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Symptoms of endometrial cancer include vaginal bleeding or discharge unrelated to menstruation, vaginal bleeding after menopause, difficult or painful urination, pain during sexual intercourse, and pain in the pelvic area.","source_span":"Vaginal bleeding or discharge not related to menstruation (periods). - Vaginal bleeding after menopause. - Difficult or painful urination. - Pain during sexual intercourse. - Pain in the pelvic area."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Endometrial cancer does not usually show up in Pap test results, so a sample of endometrial tissue must be removed and examined under a microscope.","source_span":"Because endometrial cancer begins inside the uterus, it does not usually show up in the results of a Pap test. For this reason, a sample of endometrial tissue must be removed and checked under a microscope to look for cancer cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","statement":"Dilatation and curettage (D&C) is a procedure in which the cervix is dilated and a spoon-shaped instrument called a curette is inserted into the uterus to remove tissue samples from the inner lining.","source_span":"A procedure to remove samples of tissue from the inner lining of the uterus. The cervix is dilated and a curette (spoon-shaped instrument) is inserted into the uterus to remove tissue."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is endometrial cancer?","answer":"Endometrial cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the endometrium, the lining of the uterus."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What conditions make up metabolic syndrome, a risk factor for endometrial cancer?","answer":"Metabolic syndrome includes extra fat around the abdomen, high blood sugar, high blood pressure, high triglyceride levels, and low HDL (high-density lipoprotein) levels in the blood."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How is tamoxifen related to endometrial cancer risk?","answer":"Endometrial cancer may develop in breast cancer patients who have been treated with tamoxifen."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Does taking estrogen combined with progesterone increase a woman's risk of endometrial cancer?","answer":"No, taking estrogen combined with progesterone does not increase the risk, whereas taking estrogen alone does."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are some common symptoms of endometrial cancer?","answer":"Vaginal bleeding or discharge not related to menstruation, vaginal bleeding after menopause, difficult or painful urination, pain during sexual intercourse, and pelvic pain."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why doesn't a Pap test usually detect endometrial cancer?","answer":"Because endometrial cancer begins inside the uterus, it does not usually show up in Pap test results, so a sample of endometrial tissue must instead be removed and examined under a microscope."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is done during a dilatation and curettage (D&C) procedure?","answer":"The cervix is dilated and a spoon-shaped instrument called a curette is inserted into the uterus to remove tissue samples from the inner lining for examination."}
